          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Lord Byron's Early Years</strong><br>\nGeorge Gordon Byron, known as Lord Byron, was born in 1788 into a noble but turbulent family lineage. His father's profligate spending and the death of his great-uncle led Byron to inherit the title of Lord at a young age. A birth defect left him with a lifelong limp, which influenced his sensitive and rebellious character.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Personal Life</strong><br>\nByron received his early education in Aberdeen, Scotland, and later attended Harrow and Trinity College, Cambridge. His school years were marked by the formation of important friendships and the beginning of his sexual liaisons, which carried into his college days along with a growing interest in literature and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Literary Successes</strong><br>\nByron's first volume of poetry, \"Hours of Idleness,\" was criticized, prompting his satirical reply, \"English Bards and Scotch Reviewers,\" which gained him public notice. His subsequent work, \"Childe Harold's Pilgrimage,\" made him an overnight sensation, depicting his own experiences in a form of a travelogue that captured the imaginations of many.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
