          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Infamy and Exile</strong><br>\nScandal surrounded Byron throughout his life. His rumored debts, numerous love affairs, and separation from his wife caused outrage and gossip. The public controversy, along with his self-exile from Britain, became as famous as his literary works, contributing to his mythologized persona.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Style and Influence</strong><br>\nByron is known for his versatile writing style, which ranges from the lyrical to the satirical. His works often reflect themes of love, adventure, and rebellion. He was a major figure of the Romantic movement and influenced many writers, including Edgar Allan Poe and Alfred, Lord Tennyson, with his depiction of the Byronic hero—a flawed, brooding, and complex character.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Interests and Actions</strong><br>\nAn advocate for social reform, Byron was involved in the political issues of the day. He used his fame and fortune to support causes such as the fight for Greek independence from Ottoman rule. His commitment to political change was as fervent as his dedication to poetry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
