          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Love Affairs and Scandals</strong><br>\nByron's many romances were notorious, including an infamous relationship with his half-sister, which led to much public scandal. His charismatic and moody personality attracted many social elites of the period, but it also contributed to his controversial reputation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Travel and Cultural Contributions</strong><br>\nTravel was a central aspect of Byron's life, influencing his cultural understanding and literary output. He spent extensive periods in Italy, Greece, and other parts of Europe, which enriched his writing and personal growth. Byron's travels not only inspired his own work but also helped spread the English literary culture across Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Lasting Impact</strong><br>\nLord Byron died in Missolonghi, Greece, in 1824 at the age of 36, while supporting the Greek War of Independence. His death was mourned internationally, and he became a symbol of self-sacrifice for a noble cause. Byron's literary legacy continues to influence writers and readers alike, and he remains a defining figure in English literature and the Romantic movement.</p>\n"
              },
